CAT = "uksiaSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
# Unit 1, LO4 to LO6: safe working, fire, emergencies.
Q = [
("Under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, which is a duty of an employee?",
 "Take reasonable care of others", ["Write the venue's risk assessment", "Buy their own safety equipment", "Train new staff in first aid"],
 "Employees must take reasonable care of themselves and anyone affected by their work, cooperate with the employer and report accidents. Risk assessments and equipment are the employer's duty."),
("Under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, which is a duty of an employer?",
 "Provide safe access and exit", ["Pay for staff SIA licences", "Employ a full-time paramedic", "Insure staff's own vehicles"],
 "Employers must keep employees and visitors safe, including safe access and egress, risk assessment, information, training and first aid facilities. Licence fees and a full-time paramedic are not legal duties."),
("What is a hazard?",
 "Something that could cause harm", ["The chance that harm will occur", "A record of an accident", "An injury that must be reported"],
 "A hazard is a potential source of harm, such as a wet floor or a used needle. Risk is the likelihood that someone will actually be harmed by it."),
("What is a risk?",
 "The chance that harm will occur", ["Something that could cause harm", "A sign warning of danger", "An unsafe act by a customer"],
 "Risk is the likelihood that a person will be harmed if exposed to a hazard, often rated high, medium or low. The hazard is the thing itself."),
("Which is a particular risk of lone working?",
 "No immediate back-up if hurt", ["Too many people to supervise", "Having to share a radio", "Longer queues at the door"],
 "Lone workers are isolated, rely on technology for back-up and may have no help if they are injured, fall ill or face violence. Check-in calls and personal alarms reduce the risk."),
("When lifting a heavy load, which is good practice?",
 "Keep the load close to your body", ["Twist to put it down quickly", "Bend your back and keep legs straight", "Lift it above shoulder height"],
 "Keep the load close, with a stable base, and avoid twisting. Bending the back with straight legs and lifting above shoulder height both raise the risk of injury."),
("You can either push or pull a heavy load. Which is safer?",
 "Push it in front of you", ["Pull it towards you", "Pull it walking backwards", "Drag it by one corner"],
 "Manual handling guidance is to push rather than pull where you can. Pushing lets you use your body weight, keep your back straight and see where you are going."),
("What does a round blue safety sign mean?",
 "You must do something", ["You must not do something", "Warning of a hazard", "Safe condition or exit"],
 "Blue circles are mandatory signs, such as 'wear gloves'. Red circles with a bar prohibit, yellow triangles warn and green signs show safe conditions."),
("What does a green safety sign show?",
 "A safe condition or escape route", ["An action that is prohibited there", "An action you must take", "A warning of danger ahead"],
 "Green signs show safe conditions such as fire exits, escape routes and first aid points. Red prohibits, blue is mandatory and yellow warns."),
("Under RIDDOR, which of these must be reported?",
 "A work-related death", ["A minor cut at work", "A customer complaint", "A refusal at the door"],
 "RIDDOR covers work-related deaths, specified injuries, occupational diseases and dangerous occurrences. The responsible person reports them to the enforcing authority."),
("Where should a minor injury at work be recorded?",
 "In the accident book", ["In the search register", "On the SIA website", "In the lost property log"],
 "Every injury at work should go in the accident book, saying who, what, when, where and how. Serious ones are also reported under RIDDOR."),
("Which three elements make up the fire triangle?",
 "Heat, fuel and oxygen", ["Heat, smoke and oxygen", "Fuel, water and air", "Spark, gas and smoke"],
 "A fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen. Remove any one of them and the fire goes out, which is how extinguishers work."),
("In the FIRE action on discovering a fire, what does the R stand for?",
 "Restrict", ["Rescue", "Run", "Report"],
 "FIRE is Find, Inform, Restrict, Evacuate or Extinguish. Restrict means closing doors and windows to limit the spread, and you never fight a fire if it puts you in danger."),
("Which class of fire involves flammable liquids such as petrol?",
 "Class B", ["Class A", "Class C", "Class F"],
 "Class B is flammable liquids such as petrol, paint and solvents. Class A is solids, Class C is gases and Class F is cooking oils."),
("Which class of fire involves cooking oils and fats?",
 "Class F", ["Class A", "Class D", "Class B"],
 "Class F is cooking oils and fats, which need a wet chemical extinguisher or fire blanket. Class D is burning metals."),
("Which extinguisher is the main choice for an electrical fire?",
 "CO2", ["Water", "Wet chemical", "Foam"],
 "Carbon dioxide is the primary extinguisher for electrical fires because it does not conduct electricity. Water and foam must not be used on live electrical equipment."),
("What colour band identifies a wet chemical extinguisher?",
 "Yellow", ["Red", "Black", "Cream"],
 "Extinguishers are red with a coloured band: red for water, cream for foam, black for CO2, blue for dry powder and yellow for wet chemical."),
("What is a fire marshal's role in an evacuation?",
 "Check their area is clear", ["Fight the fire until it is out", "Reset the fire alarm panel", "Lock all the fire exits"],
 "A fire marshal raises the alarm, checks their area is empty, helps anyone with evacuation difficulties and reports to the officer in charge at the assembly point."),
("What is invacuation?",
 "Moving people into a safe building", ["Moving people out of a building", "Searching a building for a suspect device", "Closing a venue for the night"],
 "Invacuation, or lockdown, brings people inside to safety, for example during an attack in the street. Evacuation moves people out, for example in a fire."),
("When dealing with a first aid incident, what should a door supervisor do?",
 "Work within their training", ["Give any medicine the person asks for", "Move the person outside straight away", "Report it only if the person complains"],
 "Know the limits of your ability, call the designated first aider or emergency services when needed, keep onlookers back, give privacy and record the injury in the accident book."),
]
